# Narrator: the treasures' second scenes (docs/ui/cutscenes.md): the line over the picture that follows each Tarokka
# treasure's own, when it is found. Second person, present tense, one or two sentences.

~ scene:sunsword_light
| Where the blade passes, the room has no shadows left to hide in. Everyone looks a little braver, and a little more tired.

~ scene:symbol_dawn
| The light spreads from it in a slow circle, over the floor and up the walls, and wherever it goes the cold takes a step back.

~ scene:tome_page
| The first page is in a hand you know from his letters. The candle beside you leans toward it, as if it, too, wanted to be read.

# The Tome of Strahd, read through (docs/ui/cutscenes.md `read:<item>`): four pictures of three captions each, played the
# first time the Tome is read. Drafted by Gemini through the ai-bridge and edited for canon (docs/story/drafts/two-cutscenes/).
# The Amber Temple vault keeps the line that he bargained to stop growing older than her: the Tome must not say it first.

~ read:tome_of_strahd
| By candlelight you read the steady script of a prince who took this valley four hundred years ago, the elder son of the warrior King Barov and Queen Ravenovia. He calls himself "a prince of the western wars" without pride or regret. He writes like a man tallying the winter stores of a fortress.

~ scene:tome_conqueror_2
| He tells how he took the valley by the sword and made the castle that bears his mother's name his seat. "I gave my father's banners their final ground, and the mud kept them." Far below the ridge his soldiers cheer. He writes that he did not turn round to look at them.

~ scene:tome_conqueror_3
| Then the wars end, and you read how the peace bored him. His armour felt heavy only once there was no one left to fight. "A kingdom won is merely a house to keep," he writes, "and I was not bred for keeping house."

~ scene:tome_brothers
| On the next pages is his younger brother Sergei, who has everything the elder lacks: ease, warmth, the gift of being loved without trying. Strahd sets this down in a level hand. "My brother wore the sun as easily as linen."

~ scene:tome_brothers_2
| Then there is Tatyana, auburn-haired and always in white, who loved Sergei and never looked toward the elder brother standing in the archway. Strahd was kind to her all that week, and says so. "Courtesy costs a ruler nothing."

~ scene:tome_brothers_3
| Here the ink changes. The first sour thought takes root on the page, and he gives it a ruler's reasons: his brother is young, the girl is young, and he is not. "Time was the only rival I could not meet on a field."

~ scene:tome_wedding
| The hand grows darker as he records a long climb into the high mountains, where a courteous old warden led him into the Amber Temple. Before a hollow amber sarcophagus a voice in the dark understood him at last. "It asked only what I wanted, and I answered without ornament."

~ scene:tome_wedding_2
| You read the terms he accepted. He would stay young for as long as he wanted anything, and keep whatever he had taken. The thing in the amber would live in him, and he in it, and he and his land would be one. The price was a life he loved. The page does not say whose.

~ scene:tome_wedding_3
| It fell due on the day he most wanted to be happy, among the white flowers Tatyana had chosen. You read how Sergei died by his hand at his own wedding feast, and never knew who struck him. "A necessary debt is best settled swiftly."

~ scene:tome_wall
| On the last leaves Tatyana runs along the high castle walls as the white mist rises from the stones. She steps off into the fog rather than be his, and leaves his reaching hand empty. "She mistook my reach for ruin, and the precipice took what was mine."

~ scene:tome_wall_2
| In the uproar below, his own guards loose their arrows at him, and the iron goes in. He does not die. You read how he woke changed in the gathering dark. "The valley and I were already one."

~ scene:tome_wall_3
| The script ends quiet and unshaken. The hand never wavers, which is how you know the man is saying it to himself: he chose every step of it. "I chose it all, and I would choose it again."
